ype/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bef182d0-f2c3-5147-8c82-4dead855f78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105418" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b875f560c133e528e4d934ae2722d14c116fac4352f6cca5089f8ec726fe3660.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,4].$ It is continuous on $(-2,4),$ and also right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=4.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[4,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(4,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [-2,4]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9da6fe68-e92e-501e-8a86-cc9ed4d2c481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461431} {:answer/id #uuid "4774def0-1649-5c3a-aafd-d5c190fb4d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4774def0-1649-5c3a-aafd-d5c190fb4d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461432} :answer-field/