nt :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667812} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9c2a0b-690e-5aa8-80c9-513fcebd098d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3785e4ee-09cd-5f43-ab4b-d1e855279826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667812} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a3df38-1159-5522-8192-378ce7bdc46c" :answer-field/key "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe40ba69-5c80-5288-8a8c-e5c1e0d1d354" :question/math-academy-id "q-105419" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c75313fbb97784b27ea0c57068e7221e0f9416ac4857b70723fd7ca949d9aba7.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and also left-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-3,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-3,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :