67824} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e4924e-4dfe-5a3f-95cf-028c0b408fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b76afc5-85e4-583a-a201-ea0fb1758f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667824} :answer-field/id #uuid "2621d3d8-ccdc-5dcc-aa55-877bda200d4c" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4b7e54f-2216-5cba-ba81-1d5ec0ebc609" :question/math-academy-id "q-108918" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y}\\right)=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49bb99f5-9999-5818-bdd3-812fc81de511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461551} {:answer/id #uuid "abbbde6a-46b0-5c8a-a35a-867cbefd832c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49bb99f5-9999-5818-bdd3-812fc81de511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461551} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ce2d44-f3a8-5077-afa5-fe22ffbe9aa5" :answer-field/key "$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462529} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84565946-12db-55cb-9543-b9e281a35249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461553} {:answer/id #uuid "76f7f31a-5fe5-53d8-be86-9a8aa1bf43ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f7f31a-5fe5-53d8-be86-9a8aa1bf43ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461554} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4701ec-9c20-5683-826f-23655d031b58" :answer-field/key "$x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462530} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1e1df6e-abab-5e94-81ce-ff2b595f6b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461555} {:answer/id #uuid "480f381b-86af-52b5-a8fc-f0e2232f7875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e1df6e-abab-5e94-81ce-ff2b595f6b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461555} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0f8dd13-47b4-5def-bd3c-ccfd3187627e" :answer-field/key "$y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462531} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e0bd703-a2da-505d-b93c-1accea187b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667830} {:answer/id #uuid "5571a264-c720-514b-a44b-52f7ad236112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e0bd703-a2da-505d-b93c-1accea187b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667830} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d87bac-ac30-5e82-b625-ab7ac87d7962" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dab8a46d-1b84-5b26-b9d0-b9d19dd8bea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109440" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+4I$ and $A+3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$” is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement “$x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$” is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement “$y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$” is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3138bb3b-2240-5c6f-8e4f-f5b183ab1ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461557} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4cb01f-fd3b-5e22-8f85-d6d31e4e2aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f4cb01f-fd3b-5e22-8f85-d6d31e4e2aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461558} :answer-field/id #uuid "3325f4bf-863d-54b9-b3e2-675ef37d6a4e" :answer-field/key "$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462532} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec37e8f-a3b6-58e1-8ac0-ebf42f83c9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461559} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1643b0-dcf4-51e2-9dee-978dbc5d77dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec37e8f-a3b6-58e1-8ac0-ebf42f83c9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461559} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9d2262-c721-59fd-8663-dd7f70aa108c" :answer-field/key "$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f3e9e53-b6e3-52c5-a18d-90e354f3cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461561} {:answer/id #uuid "167030fc-d98f-5d1e-b38a-bf7f33db7ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "167030fc-d98f-5d1e-b38a-bf7f33db7ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461562} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a39b7c6-5e57-56c4-b90a-de587ab18cd6" :answer-field/key "$v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462534} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b70b0e82-fc0d-5a6a-b4a9-70cdefb72f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667834} {:answer/id #uuid "5932c8f9-54bd-5c09-9445-4c9d41ec0f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b70b0e82-fc0d-5a6a-b4a9-70cdefb72f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667834} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d1c084-8185-572f-a62b-69a286775204" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f8f24d7-923f-567b-b949-576aeecc58aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-109506" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine out statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-3I\\,\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$”; “$v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac09a573-a23e-5936-81e2-8a4dc7b2