} :answer-field/id #uuid "f13c1106-faa0-5d5d-a220-8fa0cda4977f" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df1f63a2-8822-5dfd-aa6c-f1c9c0adcac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0376114-c41b-5238-8817-841eba5d5fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463940} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae21342-124b-5d16-ac26-43ea0aade282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0376114-c41b-5238-8817-841eba5d5fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463940} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c8c015-0913-5d4b-8128-2ce7e9902635" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b29eef7-0dba-5507-bb77-8d6b6d46d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463942} {:answer/id #uuid "7144041a-6509-5887-8f83-1d43e7ff6e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b29eef7-0dba-5507-bb77-8d6b6d46d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463942} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d17a32d-ae79-505d-9bc9-6757a44babdf" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=x{e}^{2x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464905} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f3fa1d4-4501-591a-8efe-d15597443e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463944} {:answer/id #uuid "072a40d8-5171-509c-8c5c-478de2c5e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072a40d8-5171-509c-8c5c-478de2c5e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463945} :answer-field/id #uuid "733e640b-eaa7-5cd7-9bc6-43cfad37e79b" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b95341c6-25ea-5c65-b7c7-0ba8b6e782ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667954} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dfb4ba-5b38-5768-b741-c61fb2242751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b95341c6-25ea-5c65-b7c7-0ba8b6e782ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667954} :answer-field/id #uuid "58a0d488-5edf-5b13-aa1c-3e1f5bdbf69f" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca8233-9802-5cda-93e4-fba4e6ea4800" :question/math-academy-id "q-131019" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}=x{e}^{x}({e}^{x})$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.  The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}={e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ee7c76-0edc-5280-8750-c57be156e6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463982} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7eb98f-c9cc-5ff7-882d-e64690a6b105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04ee7c76-0edc-5280-8750-c57be156e6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463982} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fd8351-5e18-5de5-94d1-03f8c50a37ae" :answer-field/key "$(-1,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a626c962-824d-5606-985e-f857cd67e651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463984} {:answer/id #uuid "d38e8240-5e70-5a8a-b490-77c795b5484d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38e8240-5e70-5a8a-b490-77c795b5484d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463985} :answer-field/id #uuid "269d9c9c-03b3-507a-ae6c-52479928bb8f" :answer-field/key "$[-2,0]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a4309a-eba9-5e24-9584-050827d306d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463986} {:answer/id #uuid "86c08b66-dfaf-5856-9af2-f0ab92ff0c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c08b66-dfaf-5856-9af2-f0ab92ff0c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463987} :answer-field/id #uuid "349fc697-8240-52e6-8d87-9502507de6df" :answer-field/key "$[0,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5bbfb4-5477-5a44-9a2e-9bc9f0ab5c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667956} {:answer/id #uuid "2cde6348-faf5-5894-9fae-3c238d286371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f5bbfb4-5477-5a44-9a2e-9bc9f0ab5c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667956} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e6d1d1-6aaa-5620-84f4-7c31679f8001" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d989e0b-f261-53e6-891f-e36529fecf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-131180" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,0]$; $[0,2]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c3c38d0-c5ee-51a5-a634-73869c8a9051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 